Message-ID: <CICE4D.5Dz@du.edu>
Date: Mon, 20 Dec 1993 16:36:12 GMT

J. S. Greenfield (greeny@top.cis.syr.edu) wrote:
: It's too much work for the plaintiff to police, but it isn't too much
: work for a carrier like CompuServe?

: How does plaintiff suggest CompuServe should prevent such infringements,
: other than shutting down all of their forums, altogether?

I don't have any inside knowledge of how CompuServe works, but when I 
ran the Unix RoundTable on GEnie, it was made very clear in the contract
that a significant part of my job (and that of my staff) was ensuring
that copyright violation uploads did *not* get cleared for download, that
that any copyright violation posts were to be removed from public view,
checked, and if unapproved, deleted.

It was also made clear that I was the one liable should copyright violations
allowed by me or my staff become an issue (that is to say, if we screwed
up, and did so so flagrantly that it became a legal issue).

It seems to me that this is the appropriate way to deal with the issue.

Frankly, the number of times this occured smacks of unprofessionalism on
the part of the staff of the forum in question.

--Dave


