Carl Kadie <cadie@eff.org> says:

>According to a Newsbytes story in clari.nb.top:
>
>'In a complaint filed in the US District Court for the Southern
>District in New York City, CompuServe is being charged with violating
>the copyright on the tune "Unchained Melody," by Alex North and Hy
>Zaret. [...] The suit charges that the copyright has been violated at
>least 690 times, based on uploads and downloads of MIDI files in the
>music forums on CompuServe.'

This could be interesting...MIDI files are not digital copies like a CD,
or even .NST and .MOD files, but are produced by cheezy MIDI equipment or
software.  Whoever created the MIDI (probably .MID or .ROL) file actually
had the sheet music (or a good ear), played it or programmed it themselves and
recorded that session as a file.  I.e., we're talking about the rights
involved in making a new version of the song, not simply duplicating
someone else's recording.  Probably not that much of a distinction, but
interesting.

>[...]
>'The Wall Street Journal quoted plaintiff's attorney Alan Shulman as
>saying "it's too much work" to go after the people uploading and
>downloading such files. "I'll never chase them all over the country."

This is very typical.  People like this simply have no concept that their
convenience could have far-reaching effects.  We're talking about a case
here that could set a precedent that could endanger about 50,000 system
operators.  All to save some trouble for a man and his lawyer.  It is of
course the person that posted the material that probably did the violating.
CIS is simply acting as a neutral conduit, and the users that downloaded
it are not intentionally infringing, since they have no way of knowing.
I really really doubt that J. Random CompuServe Users see this file and
think to themseves, "Gosh, I just know that's a copyright-violating file, so I
better grab it now before it gets removed."  I mean let's get real here.

Who knows what the court would believe though.

>That's why he wants to hold the systems that post such files liable
>for infringement. The suit asks $100,000 for each infringement.'
>[...]

This is idiotic.  The systems don't "post such files", people who use the
systems do.  Duhh.   

$100K x 690 = $69M - I don't think even Michael Jackson makes that much by
selling 690 records.  If only I were the judge in this case...a certain
greedy songwriter would be feeling very stupid at this point.

This is not to say the infringement is right.  It's not.  But this suit is
just utterly ludicrous.  Personally I think the guy's entitled to about
$700 (considering the value of a cassette, divided by an avg. of about 10
songs per tape, times number of infringements, roughly).  This $700 should
come from a suit against whoever first distributed the MIDI file.

But I'm not a lawyer so what the hell do I know.
