(Background: MIDI files are basically computerized player piano rolls.
 They define music by saying which instrument will play which note
 for how long.)

Depending on how a MIDI file was created, I would think that it could
be thought of as either a computerized version of the sheet music of a
song or as a recording of the MIDI creator's performance of a song. In
either case, (I think) you'd need permission of whoever owns the song
publishing copyright.

For example, if I remember correctly:

Michael Jackson owns the publishing rights to the Lenin & McCarthy
 song "Revolution" because he bought those rights.
Apple Records owns the copyright of the Beatle's performances of "Revolution".

If you want to use "Revolution" in a commercial and are willing to
hire a band to re-record it, you need only talk to Michael Jackson. If
you want to use the Beatle's recording of "Revolution" you must talk
to Apple Records (and I think Michael Jackson).  Similarly, if you
want to create a MIDI file of "Revolution" you need to talk to
Michael.

On a different part of the topic:

I think the key to this case will be how much editorial control
Compuserve's agents had on the archives. If the archives were managed
by no one or if they were managed by a true independent person, then
Compuserve should be OK. If not, then Compuserve is in trouble.

- Carl
