Nope, the above is the law.  So in fact, every photograph in these groups
is copyrighted unless it meets clause b.

So the next test you must assume in your analysis must be, "did the
poster have permission to post it?"

Well, in most USENET articles, the poster is the author and so there is
no problem.  The difficulty with the binary groups has nothing to do with
the fact they're about sex.  It has to do with the fact that the poster
is commonly not the copyright holder, or so it was when I examined it.

So your test is pretty simple.  Is there a note on the photo saying that
the poster is the photographer (and has model releases) or has the
permission of the photographer.

If there is no such notice, then you need to consider it as likely to be
pirate.  Brought up in court, the court will ask if a reasonable man would
have known the material to be pirate.   If the works is of professional
quality, the courts are likely to say that a reasonable man would have
known they were likely pirate.  If they are from a well known magazine, or
have phrases like "playmate" or "pet of the month" then a reasonable man
would be certain they were pirate.   In fact, when I have looked through the
group, a significant number of the pictures have even identified the source
of the photo -- you could tell they were pirate just by the description.

