I just logged into another machine and read through the groups in ASCII.

I'm afraid it wasn't even necessary to decode the pictures to find out the
groups are the same as I last recalled.

There is indeed lots of dicussion, even though it is supposed to go to
the .d group.  Nothing new there.  So one can't claim 90% of postings are
pirate if you count those.  But they aren't even supposed to be there, so
let's look at the binaries.

I found *one*, count it, *one* picture that appeared to be non-pirate from
its description.  A man posted a picture described as one of his wife.

Every other picture had no description indicating the the poster was the
author or that there was permission, but the description indicated the
material was professional porn or erotica, and as such, such a description
is necessary, because almost all professional porn or erotica requires this,
and the stuff that doesn't is almost always clearly marked.  Again, what
should a reasonable man know here?

A few of the photos had things like "playmate collection" or the names of
well known porn stars in their descriptions.  You can tell they are
pirated just from the descriptions.

Then I went to the supermodels group.  There the descriptions were even
clearer -- the photos were all of models, tv stars, etc.  Some even claimed
to come from calendars.

Folks, this is not accidental piracy.  People are *proudly* stating that
they are posting a photo from a supermodel calendar.   This is organized
piracy, and I don't see how a judge could see it any other way.

You think I'm being too strict, that merely the absence of a note of
permission isn't enough to condemn a posting.   Well, for text, the
absence isn't enough, since the default assumption is that the poster is
the author, and so notice isn't needed.  In these image groups, the default
is that the poster is not the photographer, and in fact, normally if the
poster is the photog, they proclaim it.

Imagine it for software.  What would you think of a posting in a software
binaries group with no note about who the author is, or a note saying,
"Here's a nice spreadsheet package I found lying around."  Would you feel
safe carrying a newsgroup full of that?
