I'm sure you still have the analysis on hand of when you pulled the group,
so I encourage you to post it.  I could use this data when I speak with
faculty.

As an aside, I spoke with my spouse who has handled demands for the removal
of publications from the Carnegie Library of Pittsburgh.  According to my
spouse, in order to start a review of the "appropriateness" of a publication,
a form is filled out.  On of the first questions is "Have you read this
publication?"  If the complaintant says "No", they will not accept the
complaint.  (In the case of a book they ask "Have you read this book cover
to cover").

Frankly, this is important.  People making claims that they "know" what a
book or magazine contains without having read the book or magazine are more
common than you might realize.  If I, as a staff member, were to go before
a faculty committee with a complaint about a publication, such as a newsgroup,
I surely would want to have all my ducks lined up in a row.  Telling the
committee that I (or the complaintant) hadn't even read the publication would
make me look rather foolish.

I feel that at a public (or as in the University of Pittsburgh, we are
private but "state related") University a staff member, such as myself can't
just pull a newsgroup without guidance from a faculty committee.  I have
been in contact with my management and have asked for such guidance.

However, I am also being careful about this "copyright" business.  It sounds
like a potentially "slipperly slope" that could suck in quite a bit of the
USENET.  While some may believe that the line is clear and that groups such
as "soc.men" are very distinct from "apb.*", I'm not so sure that this is
true.  Before I present material to people who have the authority to remove
groups, I am gathering as much information as possible.  (I thank all of you
for so freely giving this to me).

> So I would welcome your analysis.  Please go through the binaries in your
