rules above.

Are you now disconnecting from the USENET? ;-)  I'm certainly not!

This is one of the "slippery slopes" I was talking about.

Many replies on USENET (like the one you are reading now) include most, if not
all of the original text.  Is this a violation of the original, undeclared
copyright?  How much may you quote and still be withing "fair use"?  I'm not
sure that any of this has truely been addressed.

Is posting to USENET a defacto declaration of placing the work in the public
domain?  If so, "sez who"?.  I never gave away any of the rights to my
articles.  Yet there are companies who publish my USENET articles on CD-Rom
without my permission.  I never gave them permission to do this and don't do
so now.  (Why isn't anyone "up in arms" about this?)

The fact that it's 'done all the time' makes no difference to me!

If quoted articles and CD-roms are "copyright violations" USENET is in big
trouble.

Indeed, taking another's work from another source is a "different" case and
one I would not tolerate here.  But I would not want to have to defend the
quoting of articles before a faculty committee either.


> So the next test you must assume in your analysis must be, "did the
> poster have permission to post it?"

Absolutely.  Any articles originating from this site should pass this test.
Those found to be pirating materials will be held responsible for their
actions.

> Well, in most USENET articles, the poster is the author and so there is
> no problem.  

Once again, this is a "slippery slope".  How do you KNOW that the poster is
the author?  I know this sounds silly, but it's important to establish.
The fact is -- if it's text, and it looks bland enough, you ASSUME that it
is the original work of the author.  If is starts with "Reposted without
Permission" you assume that it is not.

So you don't really KNOW what the situation is.  But the binary groups are
to be held to a much stricter standard than the rest of USENET.  Why?

One of the assumptions (and possibly quite valid) is that if it's a PICTURE
then the author couldn't possibly have made it and/or did not have permission
to post the picture.

But the burden of proof seems to be switched simply because we are dealing
with binary (or more exactly PICTURE) newsgroups.  In soc.men all the articles
are assumed to be owned by the author while in abp.* all the articles are
assumed to be pirated unless the poster can demonstrate otherwise?

You see, I can't possibly present arguments based upon what people "know".
Especially from people who don't even read the group(s)!  I've got to
present arguments based upon demonstratable facts.  I can't treat one
newsgroup differently from another.  It's simply wrong to do so.


> The difficulty with the binary groups has nothing to do with
> the fact they're about sex.

You are mistaken if you believe that all the binary groups are about sex or
rather "erotica".  Most of the binary groups that are carried here are not
about erotica at all.  Unless there's something sexual about sounds of Bart
Simpson!

