One difference between Compuserve and the phone company or post office in
this case is that they provide editorial approval, for lack of a better
term.  Uploads are not immediately available, they have to be approved
first.  If that approval is nothing more than a virus check, fine, but 
it's still an approval.  I suspect that the arguments in this case will
make some references back several months to the Prodigy case, where they 
were sued for making a post available.  They read the post, then approved
it, which could potentially be considered publishing.  If people were
sending these files via e-mail, Compuserve would almost certainly be in
the clear, as there is an expectation that they will _not_ read personal 
email messages.  


