dweinste@solaris.carl.org (Dave Weinstein) writes:

[...]
>I both agree and disagree. CompuServe isn't acting as a carrier in this
>case (unlike the various distributed networks, like Fido and Usenet), they
>are providing a specific service (and I believe they may claim compiliation
>copyright on the contents, I know GEnie did).
[...]

If Compuserve claims a compilation copyright, in my nonlawyer opinion,
they are going to lose. If the material not managed at all, then it
doesn't qualify for a compilation copyright. If it is managed by a
editorially independent archivist, then the archivist (not Compuserve)
would own the compilation copyright. But if they own the copyright,
then the archivist must be doing work for hire, and thus very unlikely
to be considered editorially independent.

This doesn't mean that the archivists aren't "independent contractors"
for tax purposes, but I can't see they could be considered editorially
independent for liability purposes.

By analogy, newspapers work with freelance writers and photographers
but are jointly liable for copyright infringement and libel.

