Like it or not, he's right.

'Tis like a bookstore owner who gets a shipment of books with "Stolen Dave
Dimbert Cartoons" emblazoned on the cover.  What is your standard of care 
as a bookseller in that case?  I think you're required to look inside, and 
if indeed you find that they ARE stolen Dave Dimbert cartoons, you must 
refuse to sell the book or bear the consequences.  On the other hand, I 
also believe that a bookseller would not be found liable if hidden inside of 
a book on his shelf named, oh, "C Language programming" there was a stolen 
piece of the Unix operating system code - - at least not until someone told 
the bookseller that there was a problem with that particular book and he 
or she refused to remove it.

In point of fact, the net has such a "notification" mechanism.  Its called 
a cancel message.

Now, what if as a bookseller you have an area of your store for "Cartoon
Books".  And, over the space of a couple of years, it becomes established
that the majority (in fact, nearly all) of the things which your store 
carries in that section have been stolen from other places.  I believe you
now have a <higher standard of care> with regards to that section, as you
are aware of <organized> copyright infringement at your establishment.  Thus,
you end up screening every book which goes into that section.  Or do you?  At 
some point you say "screw this, its not worth the work" and dump the section,
no?

Usenet sites are very similar to those bookstores, and news admins are very
similar to bookstore owners.  In fact, it is almost a complete analog -- 
the net is a giant printed (posted) material distribution system, and in a 
sense is a huge conglomeration of bookstores (sites), each of which carries 
different sections of books (groups), each of those groups has a selection
of books (postings), and each of those bookstores (sites) is presided over 
by a bookstore owner (news admin) who decides which categories of books 
(newsgroups) to carry and distribute to others and on what terms.

Bookstores are generally not responsible for misclassifying of material 
(it happens), especially when the publisher misclassifies it themself.  
ABPE, ABPS and ABPC are special case classifications, as it is established
that those areas contain primarily, and in fact almost exclusively, 
stolen material.  Since you've read this, you're now on notice of that fact.

What you do about it as a bookstore owner is up to you.  "Do nothing" is a
choice, but one which I believe carries liability with it.

(I'm assuming Ripco.Com carries abpe, of course.  If not then I don't
understand your commentary.)

