HALIFAX CITY REGIONAL LIBRARY PRESS RELEASE

For Immediate Release
December 17, 1993

Halifax City Regional Library Restores Controversial Boston Globe Issue
to Its Shelves

The Halifax City Regional Library is returning an issue of the Boston
Globe to its shelves containing an article with information
on the Karla (Teale) Homolka manslaughter case.

Publication or distribution of material on the contents of the case
has been prohibited by a nation-wide publication ban.

By returning the issue to its shelves, the library has decided to 
follow normal procedures for incoming periodicals in the Reference
Department.

The Halifax Library had previously removed pages from its copy of
the Boston Globe under legal advice.  The library is acting on the
basis of new information - legal advice provided to the McGill
University Library.  The advice stated in part that "merely placing
the newspapers on the shelves of the periodicals room would not
constitute a prohibited act."  The same legal opinion later noted
that "it is virtually impossible for the University to monitor the
content of each and every periodical it receives each day."

