FSP sites can be and are logged.  Trust me on this -- I have the client 
and server code here.  Anyone who labors under the assumption that IP 
traffic cannot be traced is provably wrong, and will eventually get a 
seriously ugly surprise.

If the traffic comes through MCSNet or any other site which is watching 
the store that surprise is likely to come sooner rather than later.
Further, since most "real" sites keep regular backups (sometimes in a
fashion that's not obvious to the user) it is <highly> dangerous to do this
kind of trafficking.  For instance, my backup tapes are in a bank vault.
If I get a subpoena for someone who the law wants to look over, I can go
get the tapes from the bank vault and reconstruct nearly <every file> you 
have had in your directory over the last three years.  The likelihood that 
you will be convicted and go to prison if you do this kind of thing on 
MCSNet machines is rather high.

The net is not as unaccountable as people might believe.

All FSP happens to be is a protocol which uses UDP packets on an
"artificial" flow-controlled basis for transport.  Its much lower load 
than the traditional FTP protocol due to this, which means that servers can
support hundreds of simultaneous users without burying the serving machine.
However, performance is directly related to the number of users, as the 
server deliberately limits its transmission rate so it doesn't bury the
serving system.

They are certainly not "untraceable", and they certainly <do> leave trails 
all over the place both in the server and client machines.  Anything which
creates sockets has an entry in the netstat tables while the software is
running, and it is easy to get the peer name on any packet which arrives on
a given machine (ie: the IP number it came from).  If the server system is
running any sort of authentication code on their IP stack (not difficult
to do) you have a log which the user is <completely> unaware of - this
is trivial to insert into the system accounting routines on SVR4, and
not much harder on the source-available BSD releases.  Given that you have 
the calling and answering machine addresses, which is all you need to 
send the feds over for a peek.

Don't believe the above poster for a second.  Using the Internet to trade
"warez", "codez" and kiddie porn is a good way to go to prison for a long,
long time.

