                             Enjoy.

              Tolerance in Cyberspace: A case study.

Most evenings last year several of my friends would join me late
at night to watch the Rush Limbaugh television show.  Some were
Republicans and some were not.  But we all watched and laughed
and discussed.  Funny and thought provoking -- that is Rush.  
Over the summer we got bored watching the reruns.  I listened to
his radio show when I could but we missed the comraderie that the
TV show engendered.  As September 13 (the day the new season
began) approached we began to get excited.  I decided to host a
party at my house to commemorate the event.  I sent out an
invitation to the "graduates" mailgroup in psychology.  The
invitations follows.

= = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = 
                    Ta Da Lup  Ta Da Lup  Ta Da Lup 
 
                             This is a 
 
                        Limbaugh Party Update 
 
 
Greetings conversationalists across the fruited plain.  Monday, 
Sept. 13, will be day number 237 of the hostage crisis (day 256 
for the rich or dead).  To help you cope with the Raw Deal, I 
will be hosting a welcome back Rush party. 
 
Yes, that's right, the Doctor of Democracy presents his first 
lecture of the semester at the Limbaugh Institute for Advanced 
Conservative Studies.  With talent on loan from God and half his
brain tied behind his back (just to make things fair), Rush will
offer his unique blend of irreverent humor, information, and 
commentary.  The dominant media culture will quake once again as
equal time arrives.  It'll be more fun than a human should be 
allowed to have. 
 
Festivities will begin during Monday Night Football (49ers vs 
Browns).  A tax (uh, contribution) of $1 will be collected to 
defray the cost of chips, soft drinks, and adult beverages 
(that's beer for those of you in Rio Linda).  A tax (uh, 
contribution) exemption will be given to those who arrive wearing
a Deficit Spending Awareness Ribbon.  Liberals please add $14.95
to show your true compassion for the less fortunate. :) 
 
Seating is VERY limited.  So please R.S.V.P. (a little French 
lingo there).  Until then, believe in yourself and be of good 
cheer. 
 
                                   Tim Buckley 
 
P.S. As a bonus I present an environmentalist whacko football 
pick for the Monday night game (49ers vs Browns).  The Browns
will win easily.  After all, as the 49ers traveled from east to
west they destroyed the pristine beauty of the continent.  They
then proceeded tear apart the mountains of California in search
of gold which they kept and used for their own selfish purposes. 
On the other hand, brown is an earth tone - soft and
nonthreatening.  And Cleveland never wins anything anyway and
that is not fair. 

Browns 27 49ers 17 

= = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = 
Well that was it.  It seemed pretty simple to me.  The mailgroup
is used for both official and unofficial announcements.  Parties
are one of the most common types of announcements circulated.

The response was swift and, I suppose, predictable.

Response 1: "If Rush has half his brain tied behind his back then
he's got nothing left to use for his talk.  Or perhaps you don't
need a brain to spout conservative dogma.  Dan Quayle is
certainly evidence for this... "
 
Well, it could have been worse I thought.  But I was disappointed
to see that it was not only sent to me but posted to "soapbox", a
department wide forum that most professors, clericals, and grads
have access to.  I had expected some hate mail but had not
expected to get into a public debate.  I didn't reply.

Response 2: "Rush Limbaugh is a vile, evil, man.  To call him
racist and sexist would be to grossly understate his lack of
feeling for anyone who is not rich, white, and male.  His
contempt for those in the margins of society is surpassed only by
his arrogance ("With talent on loan from god"?). 
 
I'd prefer that I were not barraged with information about the
time and place of your personal hatefests."
 
Hmmmmm, a personal HATEFEST?  This was also posted publicly.  Now
I was getting a bit annoyed.  I don't think that anyone who knows
me considers me to be a hateful person.  I did not take kindly to
being attacked this way.  Still I held my tongue.

Response 3:"OUTSTANDING TIM !!! 
 
I am a fellow Ditto-head and I am ALWAYS happy to hear about 
others.  Rush is truly an exceptional person and everybody
(including our leftist Presidential regime) will eventually
realize this.  Your party is a great way to "spread the word" --
don't know if I can attend but I will sure try. 
 

A private reply.  This was what I had been hoping for.  But
then.....

Response 4: "I agree with **** emphatically and would greatly
appreciate it if you would not put any mention of the Great White
Worm on my e-mail either."

And 

Response 5: "Unfortunately, I am unable to enjoy Rush due to my
gender, race, and socio-economic status, etc, so I will not be
attending the festivities."

And so it went.  Some positive, most negative.  There were some
who came to my defense privately and those comments were much
appreciated.  But publicly I was still a scoundrel.  Finally I
responded with the following post on tolerance.

= = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = 
On Tolerance, Offensiveness, and Rush Limbaugh 
 
Much is written today about tolerance.  Given the value placed on
it in our society, people go to great lengths to be perceived as
tolerant.  Conversely, to be labeled as intolerant is one of the
worst criticisms that can be rendered on a human being.  But who
makes the rules as to what is tolerable and what is not?  Surely
not everything can be tolerated.  Murder can not be tolerated. 
Torture can not be tolerated.  Neither can we tolerate robbery,
nor slavery, nor many other activities.  In some sense our very
society is defined by what is tolerated and what is not
tolerated. 
 
It is easy to see that the activities above are destructive to 
our society.  But what about the less severe behaviors and 
opinions that shape our society?  How tolerant should we be? 
This is no easy question to answer.  The current discussion over
the use of the "graduates" mailgroup is an example of the 
complexity of this puzzle.  

Some felt that my invitation about Rush Limbaugh should not be 
tolerated.  Partially because some consider him intolerant.  This
seems somewhat circular to me even if the statement about Rush is
true.  It would seem that those tolerant people who wish to 
silence him are themselves showing a degree of intolerance.  In a
society in which we prize free speech this seems 
counterproductive and dangerous.  It dredges up images of a 
thought police. 
 
Does tolerance imply agreement?  Of course not!  Does tolerance 
require one to listen?  Of course not!  But tolerance DOES 
require that others be allowed to listen.  And this requirement 
implies that when a group is addressed some might be forced, at 
first, to listen to something that they don't want to hear. 
After all, how will one know what one likes if one does not take
the chance to listen to a variety of voices?  And even if one has
heard an opinion before and is now certain about what one does 
not want to hear, shouldn't others be given the same chance to 
make up their minds?  To allow others this opportunity requires 
us to be tolerant even when we do not want to be. 
 
Someone objected to my invitation because he thought it should 
have been clear to me that others would be offended.  This 
objection was based on the writer's perceptions of the base rates
of attitudes in the department.  The implication was that my 
perceptions should closely mirror his, and thus, I should have 
known better.  The problem with this is that in a free society 
people are frequently going to be offended.  Should we never say
anything that could offend anyone? 
 
Let's see what would be allowed to cross our desks (or computers)
were we to adopt this approach. 
 
My invitation is out because it might offend some liberals or 
others.  That's ok, right? 
 
A post asking for rides to an abortion rights rally is gone 
because it would offend right-to-lifers.  Is that ok? 
 
A post announcing a special fellowship for minorities or women is
out because it offends white males.  Is that ok? 
 
A post announcing a halloween party is out because it offends 
some christians who think that halloween is the devil's work.  Is
that ok? 
 
A post announcing a Christmas party is out because Jews find it 
offensive.  Is that ok? 
 
A post announcing a happy hour is out because it offends 
recovering alcoholics.  Is that ok? 
 
Is a post inappropriate if it offends a small minority or only if
it offends a fairly large minority?  Or only if it offends a 
majority?  And on what basis is its offensiveness to be judged? 
On its intent to offend?  On its potential to offend?  Or, only 
if offense is taken? 
 
Just where will all this lead?  If we are permitted to say only 
those things that are certain to be inoffensive to all, what will
be left to say?  Precious little, I'm afraid.  No humor, no 
passion, no humanity. 
 
This type of thinking is particularly troubling in a department 
of scientists who pride themselves on objectivity.  One can be 
called a racist, sexist, or homophobe just for asking a question
and carrying out a study designed to answer it.  How is this 
conducive to good science?  Haven't we learned anything from the
trials of Copernicus who dared to question the teachings of the 
Church regarding the solar system?  If our primary goal is to 
remain inoffensive, how will we advance our craft?  Though we 
should not seek to be offensive, we cannot let political 
correctness be our god.  If, as scientists, we shut our eyes, 
hold our ears, or keep our mouths closed, we will make ourselves
and our work irrelevant to the human experience. 
 
On Rush Limbaugh. 
 
He has been called many names: evil, sexist, homophobic, hateful,
vile, racist, etc.  I won't address these directly and neither
will I address the implication that those who would enjoy him are
either ignorant or of the same ilk.  But I will provide some
information that might shed some light on him for those who are
unfamiliar with him. 
 
He is an entertainer first and foremost.  His earlier shows were
more entertainment and less politics.  He has evolved into what 
he is.  Whatever that is. 
 
He is irreverent, informative, and funny.  I personally find his
humor much less offensive than much of what I've seen on Saturday
Night Live, In Living Color, or Beavis and Butthead to name a few
other irreverent shows.  Your mileage may vary. 
 
Despite his claims to the contrary he does have a political 
impact. 
 
This week (Wed. Sept. 8) on a stage at the Capitol, Rep. Inhofe 
of Oklahoma credited two Americans with helping to effect the 
beginnings of a major reform movement in the House of 
Representatives: Rush Limbaugh and Ross Perot.  This occurred on
a stage filled with Democrats and Republicans alike.  Inhofe was
the leader of the movement.  The effect will be to change the way
bills are brought to the House floor for a vote.  It will make it
difficult for members to hide behind the cover of secret ballots.

Forty-two percent of his radio audience is female.  His radio 
audience is over 20 million a week and over four million during 
any 15 minute segment of his three hour radio show.  That means 
over 8 million women listen to his show each week. 
 
Twenty-three percent of his audience says that they disagree with
most of his views.  But they listen to him. 
 
His book is one of the fastest selling nonfiction books in the 
history of publishing.  Over 2.5 million copies sold.  I'm sure 
not all who read him agree with him.  But they read him. 
 
His newsletter sells over 350,000 copies per month.  That's twice
as much as The Nation and the New Republic together.  I'm sure 
that not all his subscribers agree with him on everything.  But 
they sure do read him. 
 
His television show was the third highest rated in nighttime TV.
It followed only Nightline and Leno in the ratings.  In every 
outlet in which he was paired against Leno or Letterman he came 
out on top.  Viewers may not agree with him.  But they watch him.
 
I never asked people to agree with him.  I just asked people if 
they wanted to watch, listen, and laugh.  We can argue after the

show. :) 
 
Tim 

= = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = = 

The response to this post was quite different and quite positive. 
My favorite response was this:

"Hi, I don't know you and I refuse to watch Rush because my
