Unfortunately this is not a valid legal defense.  In fact, music rights
organizations appear to use this very principle to intimidate people into
paying for their services.  They refuse to publish a list of songs for
which they handle the rights.  They claim they handle so many, that it
isn't practical to list which ones they handle.  So you have to pay them,
just in case, they might handle the rights to one of the songs you might
play.  So you usually end up paying them, even if you never play a song
for which they handle the rights, just in case.

I've heard that they will to tell people for an individual song if they
handle the rights.  But even then, the FUD principle is used, because
even if they told you yesterday they didn't handle the rights for that
song, they might have them tommorrow.  And the fact they told you
yesterday they didn't handle the song isn't a defense if they start
to handle the song rights tomorrow.

The real question isn't if something is copyrighted, since Berne, you have to
assume that basically any recent thing that can be copyrighted, is.  Rather
the real question is if the person posting/uploading/etc has the rights
to distribute it?  The next question is if that person doesn't have the
rights, who is liable?

> The real problem is that it is the _sender_ not the transmitter who must
> ultimately be responsible for these (all to frequent) violations. Do we
> expect Xerox to station a staff member at each photocopy machine to check
> that copyrighted material is not being duplicated? Do the cops collar 
> the UPS man for delivering some illicit substance? Do we bust the phone
> company if you say something naughty on the phone to a friend?

Usually it hinges on the question of control.  Does Xerox control, or
should they be expected to control, what is photocopied on those machines.
Obviously the photocopier machines installed at Xerox, Inc's headquarters
are under their control. So I expect them to be held liable for copyright
infringement by their employees on those machines.  On the other hand,
Xerox doesn't have control, nor is expected to have control, over Xerox-
brand photocopier machines installed and operated by non-Xerox people.
So I would not expect Xerox to be held liable if that machine was used
to infringe copyright by someone.

Just because it would be easier to sue Xerox, rather than hunting around
the country for the actual infringers, doesn't make Xerox liable for
copyright infringment that happens using Xerox manufactured photocopier
machines by non-Xerox employees.

BTW, if you happen to be a Xerox(TM) lawyer sorry about taking your name
in vain.  I would appreciate you sending the letter about Xerox being
a registered trademark directly to me.  I would like to get it framed :-).

Of course, the music industry is a completely different beast.  They
felt they were burned by the jukebox compromise fourty-or-so years ago,
and have been vicious ever since.  It would be very amusing to see the
music industry's reaction if CompuServe and BBS's were ruled to be
the electronic equivalent of a jukebox.
