A story, Karl.  I once was a member of a jury, empanelled to
hear the case of a man charged with being an accessory to the
theft of some stained glass church windows.  How so?  He owned
a car.  A friend called him, and asked to borrow it.  But since
his friend lacked a driver's license, he volunteered to drive
the car.  The friend and his companions loaded some stained
glass windows (small, about basement-window size) into the
trunk, and they drove to a series of antique stores.

At one of the stores, the proprietor believed that the windows
being offered for sale were the same ones that had been reported 
stolen the day before.  She told the men that she had no use
for them, but suggested that they try another store, on the
south side of town.  They indicated that they would, and left. 
She called the police, to alert them of her suspicions.

The first policeman on the scene was a plainclothesman, who
also happened to be familiar with antiques.  He walked up to
the car, as it was parked in front of the second antique store,
and engaged the driver in conversation.  The "leader" of the
little group had gone inside to plead his case.  The cop asked
what they were doing.  The driver explained that they had some
stained glass windows they were trying to sell.  The cop
expressed some interest, and was shown the articles in
question.  

At about that moment, a number of squad cars pulled up, and the
cops in them jumped out with guns drawn.  The men were
arrested, and the defendant appeared in court before us,
entering a plea of "not guilty" of the charge of being an
accessory to the crime of theft of property.

His defense, as best I can recall it, was that he had no
knowledge or expectation that the windows had been stolen.

Credible evidence was presented as to why this might have been
so.  We, the jury, deliberated for about an hour and a half or
so, and returned a verdict of "not guilty" with respect to the
driver.  We believed that the police had not met the
requirement that they establish his complicity "beyond a
reasonable doubt".

Moral:  On the face of it, this guy might have looked as guilty
as sin, but once all the evidence was in, and the constraints
of the law explained, we had no course but to return a "not
guilty" verdict.  You know what they say, "judge not lest ye be
judged."  
