In article <2fb61f$qp0@Mercury.mcs.com> karl@MCS.COM (Karl Denninger) writes:

| However, a court could do so, by putting said offender in prison for a 
| few years.

I have to think that this is unlikely at best. If someone were posting
them with a BBS ad appended (Russ 'n' Eddie come to mind) you might get
some action, but postings from a non-commercial site are unlikely to
result in much legal action.

The reason is the "fair use" clause, allowing a copying for
non-commercial purposes when used for "personal reference" or
"research." Someone could claim the copy is for one or both of the above
reasons and at least make the issue complex, expensive to try, and
attractive to various free speech and civil rights groups.

If someone were regularly posting all or most of the pix from a
magazine, there could be a claim of actual diminished sales, but absent
that I think the risk is a fine and some legal fees, rather than prison.
In the real world the result is likely to be an agreement not to
persist, rather than any trial at all. The courts are pretty full to
prosecute a case where there's no profit made or lost, and most of the
magazines rely heavily on the freedom of publication, and might not want
to be seen as infringing on other's rights.

I make no claim that this is a valid legal opinion, just my thoughts on
the way the world works. An individual posting a few pix is unlikely to
get a hassle, and the legal issues of copyright infringument are not
nearly as clear in private use as in commercial cases.
