Remember, the point of this exercise is (1) not to get sued, and (2) not to
lose if you do get sued.  IF and when you get sued then you're playing the 
"how do I prevail" game.  Any person with intelligence that is responsible for
a site is trying to stack the deck in the event that happens <up front>, 
mitigate the risk of it happening in the first place, or at least not damage 
their position before they get started.

(1) is reduced <dramatically> by not carrying the stolen-postings-are-c00l
groups.  It plays well to someone who is truly pissed off about your system
having the material on it if you can demonstrate that you try not to have
it happen there through system policy and the operational integrity of your
organization.  (2) is reduced by trying to be proactive about (1) to the
extent possible.

If you, on the other hand, take the "its fair use!" banner, or the "its
not my problem what my system is used for" banner, you could end up in the
same position that some folks on the net were a few years ago -- and find
out that someone wants to "teach you a lesson".  Now, when someone gets
pissed off at you about a violation, they're going to be <really> pissed
off, since you believe you have a "right" to carry that stolen material.

I'll <never> make a statement or conduct business in a fashion that
suggests that I think I have a "right" to purvey stolen material, because
I don't.  As a software publisher as well as a service provider I am on the
side of the table which is interested in NOT seeing piracy take place.

When in front of a judge, image and your intent are <the> things you want to
be concerned with.  If you look like a clean-cut businessperson who has made
every reasonable attempt to prevent a problem, and it happens anyway, you're 
much less likely to get screwed to the wall than if you look like a 
long-haired dope-smoking everything-is-free-and-I-have-a-right-to-this 
type.  In fact, the few times that corporations are found liable for these
kinds of things it is usually <precisely> because they know about the
problem and have deliberately ignored it.  Witness the Ford Pinto and Chevy
Truck incidents, or any one of the big corporate injury scandles for some 
examples.

I <know> that sex sells.  Hell, let's face it -- the entire reason that 
this is good for an uproar is that <sex sells> and people want it.  I am
certainly interested in this material <if it is not infringing> being here.
